test_exectgt — execve target program
====================================

The post-execve image that `test_execve` invokes via `pm_execve`. When
this binary's entry point runs, it means PM's `pm_handle_execve`
successfully (a) dropped the caller's old VMAs + pages, (b) installed
per-PT_LOAD VMAs from this ELF, (c) copied this binary's segments
into the calling process's address space via `svc_vm_copy_to`, (d)
built the System V argv/auxv init stack, and (e) rewrote the saved
exception frame so the calling thread's next ring-3 transition lands
right here.

To prove every step worked, we read `argv[1]` (which `test_execve`
sets to `"ok"`) and print it out. A bare `execve: ok\n` line means
the entire pipeline succeeded; anything else is a regression.

    module oskit.bin.test_exectgt

    import oskit.ulib.{puts, exit, exit_with}


    main(argv: []string)
        if len(argv) < 2
            puts("execve: bad argc=")
            // Print argc inline rather than pulling in print_idec.
            var n = len(argv)
            if n < 10
                var b: [1]byte
                b[0] = byte('0' + n)
                puts(string(&b[0], 1))
            else
                puts(">=10")
            puts("\n")
            exit_with(1)
            return

        if argv[0] != "test_exectgt"
            puts("execve: bad argv0=")
            puts(argv[0])
            puts("\n")
            exit_with(2)
            return

        puts("execve: ")
        puts(argv[1])
        puts("\n")
        exit()
